  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCurrently, there is an upper shortwave trough sliding across \nsouthern Canada with weak upper ridging across much of the central \nand southern CONUS. A weak upper shortwave is over the Southwest US, \nkeeping our area in southwesterly flow aloft. At the surface, a low \nis over northern Ontario with a cold front draping down through\nthe Upper Mississippi Valley, connecting to a low in eastern \nColorado. An increased pressure gradient remains over Kansas, \nresulting in breezy southerly winds. Sustained winds are between\n15 to 20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph especially in central KS. \nSlightly below normal high temperatures continue with highs \nrising into the 60s. As we move into tonight and Monday morning,\nlows will be near normal with readings in the 40s. \n\nFor Monday-Tuesday, the aforementioned weak upper shortwave over the \nSouthwest US will propagate towards the Central Plains. Given the \ndry air from the surface through the mid-levels, rain shower chances \nare unlikely with this activity. Near normal high temperatures are \nforecast with highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s.  \n\nFor Wednesday and beyond, upper ridging will build over the Plains \nas a deep upper trough digs off the coast of California. This will \npromote a warming trend along with thickness increasing and above \nnormal 850 mb temperatures. Highs will rise back into the 70s with \nabove normal temperatures persisting through Sunday. Rain chances \nmay return to the forecast next weekend as the upper trough \ntraverses toward the region. There is still quite a bit of \nuncertainty but at this time looks like there is a 30% chance for \nrain in western KS into central KS. Stay tuned.",
